<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antiochus II Theos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 286–246 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Antiochus II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antiochus II Theos was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> from 261 to 246 BCE. He fought the Second Syrian War against Ptolemy II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, which ended with his marriage to Ptolemy's daughter Berenice, and recovered cities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>. The Milesians called him Theos, 'god', for freeing them from the tyrant Timarchus, and the rock edicts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> name him among the Hellenistic kings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antiochus-ii-theos.jpg" alt="Antiochus II Theos" width="250" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antiochos_II_Theos_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
